597	/// Which insured item answers a reported damage, and when the office refuses
598	/// the claim instead. An insured item answers at most one damage: a policy
599	/// covering one sword cannot answer two sword damages. The office enforces
600	/// that by striking the item it assigns off the list of those still
601	/// unanswered, so each damage is assigned an item of its own and a policy
602	/// covering two swords answers two sword damages.
603	///
604	/// Both ways a damage can fail to be assigned are refused in the same words,
605	/// naming the type: a policy that covers no sword at all and a policy whose
606	/// only sword has already answered an earlier damage are, from the claim's
607	/// side, the same objection -- this damage has no insured item to be settled
608	/// against, so the office declines to cover it and refuses the claim whole.
609	/// The office is asked only for a refusal the CLI can report and exit on, and
610	/// it has no finer ruling to make between the two grounds. A ruling that did
611	/// distinguish them -- different wording per ground, or refusing only the
612	/// surplus damage rather than the whole claim -- would be the change that
613	/// splits this into two objections.
614	fn insured_item_answering<'a>(
615	    unanswered: &mut Vec<&'a Item>,
616	    damage: &Damage,
617	) -> Result<&'a Item, String> {
618	    let position = unanswered
619	        .iter()
620	        .position(|item| item.item_type == damage.item_type);
621	    match position {
622	        Some(position) => Ok(unanswered.remove(position)),
623	        None => Err(format!(
624	            "item not covered by the policy: {}",
625	            damage.item_type
626	        )),
627	    }
628	}
