638	/// How much of the reported damage the office admits before the deductible --
639	/// which of its reimbursement clauses governs, and what that clause admits.
640	/// The office has two: damage to an item enchanted at or above the
641	/// reimbursement level is admitted at half, and damage to an item made of
642	/// dragon material is reimbursed in full. Only the enchantment clause appears
643	/// as a branch here, and that is the whole clause set faithfully, not a
644	/// clause left out:
645	///
646	/// - Full reimbursement IS the dragon-material outcome, and it is also what
647	///   the office admits when no clause reduces the damage. The two clauses
648	///   therefore differ in which items they name, not in what they pay, so a
649	///   separate dragon-material branch would compute the same amount the
650	///   fall-through already computes.
651	/// - Where both clauses name the same item, the halving clause governs: a
652	///   dragon-material sword enchanted at the reimbursement level is admitted at
653	///   half, not in full. Ranking the enchantment clause first is what makes the
654	///   collapse above sound, because it leaves the dragon clause reachable only
655	///   where it agrees with the fall-through.
656	///
657	/// So this stays one decision with one discriminating predicate. A third
658	/// clause, or a clause whose share were neither half nor full, would break the
659	/// collapse and force the clause set to become visible structure.
660	///
661	/// The reimbursement level is the claim side's own threshold, set higher than
662	/// the level at which the premium side charges a risk surcharge: the office
663	/// surcharges an enchantment of 5 but only halves its reimbursement from 8.
664	fn reimbursable_damage_of(damaged_item: &Item, damage: &Damage) -> i64 {
665	    let highly_enchanted =
666	        damaged_item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_LEVEL;
667	    if highly_enchanted {
668	        return admitted_damage_of(damage.amount as f64 * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT);
669	    }
670	    damage.amount
671	}
672	
673	/// An admitted damage is rounded down -- the MHPCO's favor for money it pays
674	/// out, the mirror of the rounding up a premium gets in `total_premium_of`.
675	/// This is the office's house-wide favor ruling, not a reimbursement clause:
676	/// it is the direction a fraction is resolved in, whatever clause produced the
677	/// fraction. Rounding sits here, at the halving clause, because that clause is
678	/// the only step of a settlement that can yield a fraction at all -- the
679	/// deductible, the insurance values and the cap are whole G throughout -- so
680	/// rounding the share the office admits and rounding the final payout are the
681	/// same figure. A clause admitting some other share would arrive here too; a
682	/// fractional deductible or cap would move the ruling to the final payout.
683	fn admitted_damage_of(admitted_share: f64) -> i64 {
684	    admitted_share.floor() as i64
685	}
